      "writtenOn": "2024-09-11T02:34:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e, but for tracking I believe dirty bits are better because they allow easy masking with the pipeline\u0027s mask of used constants.\n\nYou convinced me. I agree that the dirty bits is a necessary and simple state tracking struct. What\u0027s in my mind is:\n```\n// All known internal immediate data usages. Offset (and // size for some special \n// internal immediate data usages like arrayLengthFromUniform) are initialized to // std::max\u003cuint32_t\u003e or some invalid values.\nuint32_t VertexIndexOffset \u003d 0;\nuint32_t BufferLengthOffset \u003d invalid;\n\n// Values for each internal immediate data;\nstd::array\u003cuint32_t, 2\u003e firstIndexFirstInstance \u003d {};\nstd::vector\u003cuint32_t\u003e bufferLengths;\n\n// Or a uniform vector. Vector could reserve size after initialization. With \n// known offsets, it could store all values continuous.\n// std::vector\u003cuint32_t\u003e allValues.\n\n// Bits to represent whether pipeline need the internal immediate data, \n// initialize once and never changes again.\nBits mUsedConstants;\n\n// DirtyBits represent all known internal immediate data usages\nDirtyBits mAllInternalImmediateDataStates;\n\n```",
      "parentUuid": "0f44d4d8_f02d9d25",
      "revId": "1392ccb155b29453b7f1c581eb4f814d7ca830c0",
